From: Custom Raumfeld firmware project
Subject: [PATCH] ALSA: pcm: preserve driver timestamp in 32-bit status

The time32 status conversion copies audio_tstamp into driver_tstamp.
Audio time is relative to the PCM run; driver time uses the configured
system clock. Mixing them makes ARM32 clients schedule playback against
elapsed audio time instead of CLOCK_MONOTONIC.

Copy the driver's seconds and nanoseconds from their matching fields.

Signed-off-by: Custom Raumfeld firmware project
---
 sound/core/pcm_native.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sound/core/pcm_native.c b/sound/core/pcm_native.c
--- a/sound/core/pcm_native.c
+++ b/sound/core/pcm_native.c
@@ -1164,8 +1164,8 @@ static int snd_pcm_status_user32(struct snd_pcm_substream *substream,
 		.audio_tstamp_data = status64.audio_tstamp_data,
 		.audio_tstamp_sec = status64.audio_tstamp_sec,
 		.audio_tstamp_nsec = status64.audio_tstamp_nsec,
-		.driver_tstamp_sec = status64.audio_tstamp_sec,
-		.driver_tstamp_nsec = status64.audio_tstamp_nsec,
+		.driver_tstamp_sec = status64.driver_tstamp_sec,
+		.driver_tstamp_nsec = status64.driver_tstamp_nsec,
 		.audio_tstamp_accuracy = status64.audio_tstamp_accuracy,
 	};
 
